WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.299
Strong validation starts with a clear contract.

00:00:03.299 --> 00:00:13.198
Before processing data, define what the application expects: type, length, format, range, required fields, optional fields, allowed values, nested objects, arrays, and business rules.

00:00:13.198 --> 00:00:22.708
If the contract is vague, validation usually becomes a collection of partial checks that are hard to test and easy to bypass accidentally.

00:00:22.708 --> 00:00:25.620
Type checks alone are not enough.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:34.353
A value may be a string but still be too long, in the wrong format, or unacceptable for the current workflow.

00:00:34.353 --> 00:00:38.817
A number may parse successfully but exceed an allowed range.

00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:45.999
An array may contain the right general type but include too many items or unexpected nested objects.

00:00:45.999 --> 00:00:52.792
A boolean may be valid syntactically but not a field the user is allowed to set.

00:00:52.792 --> 00:00:55.315
Business rules complete the contract.

00:00:55.315 --> 00:00:59.391
A start date should come before an end date.

00:00:59.391 --> 00:01:03.467
A role value should come from an approved set.

00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:08.319
An account identifier should match the authenticated user or tenant context.

00:01:08.319 --> 00:01:13.171
A price, discount, or workflow state should follow the application's rules.

00:01:13.171 --> 00:01:18.023
Well-formed data can still be unsafe when it violates business meaning.

00:01:18.023 --> 00:01:22.099
Contracts should be visible to developers, testers, and reviewers.

00:01:22.099 --> 00:01:30.056
Schema definitions, shared validation functions, API specifications, TypeScript interfaces paired with runtime schemas, and documented request models all help.

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:39.179
The more explicit the contract, the easier it is to reject invalid input consistently and test the behavior when future changes arrive.
